{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"wakeless-maleficent-raptors","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`7e9e-07f7-8fe1-4e38` delivered gateway correlation headers but explicitly deferred the\nrun-level root span. The consequence is stated in that epic's Out-of-scope and is real: rebar\nopens no span per gate run, so `tracing.py`'s per-candidate spans are each their own trace root.\nA plan-review that fans out across chunk, container and verify calls therefore appears in\nLangfuse as many traces rather than one, and the gateway header id (minted at the boundary when\nnothing is recording) does not match any of them.\n\nPrior-art review of the header design flagged this as the idiomatic end state — Langfuse's own\nmodel is one trace per unit of work — and the epic's own plan-review confirmed the shipped\nbehaviour does not achieve it.\n\n## Approach\n\nOpen one rebar-owned span at each gate run boundary (`workflow/gate_dispatch.py`'s two\n`gate_config` sites), so every agent call in the run nests beneath it and the run is one trace.\n`run_identity.mint_run_identity` already prefers an active recording span's trace id over\nminting, so the correlation header and the exported spans would then carry the same value with\nno further change — that read exists precisely to make this cheap.\n\nContextvar propagation into the parallel fan-out already works (`generation._submit_ctx` runs\neach pool task under `copy_context()`), so no threading work is required.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_dispatch.py` — open and close the run span at both boundaries.\n- `src/rebar/llm/tracing.py` — the span factory, alongside the existing `candidate_span`.\n- `tests/unit/test_run_span.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: in-process unit tests against the real tracer — span parentage runs for real in-process.\n\n- Happy path: candidate spans opened inside a gate run share the run span's trace id.\n- Edge: with no tracer configured, behaviour is unchanged and nothing raises.\n- Correlation: `mint_run_identity` inside the run returns the run span's trace id, so the\n  gateway header and the exported spans agree.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Two candidate spans opened within one gate run share a trace id; checked by asserting both\n      against the run span's context.\n- [ ] Inside a gate run with a recording tracer, `mint_run_identity` returns that run span's\n      trace id rather than a minted value.\n- [ ] With no tracer configured the gate run behaves exactly as before; checked by asserting no\n      exception and an unchanged verdict shape.\n- [ ] `gate_dispatch.py` stays at or under the 800-line module cap.","id":"8772-d92c-5682-4718","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Open a rebar-owned root span per gate run so one run is one trace"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787771609645569001,"uuid":"8d5e036b-38bc-46a0-b19e-b92f2ba2bb49"}